"New upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers (https://oamonitor.ireland.openaire.eu/national/search/publication?pid=10.48550%2Farxiv.2501.02925)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; doi: 10.48550/arxiv.2501.02925\n\narXiv: 2501.02925\n\nNew upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers\n\ndescription Publication keyboard_double_arrow_right Article , Preprint 01 Jan 2025 Embargo end date: 01 Jan 2025\n\nAuthors: Hegedüs, Gábor;\n\ndoi: 10.48550/arxiv.2501.02925\n\narXiv: 2501.02925\n\nNew upper bounds for the size of set systems with restricted intersections modulo prime powers\n\n  *     * Summary\n\n  *     * Subjects\n\n  *     * Metrics\n\nAbstract\n\nLet $q=p^α$ be a fixed prime power, $k\\geq 2$ be an integer. We give a new upper bound for the size of $k$-wise $q$-modular $L$-avoiding $L$-intersecting set systems, where $L$ is any proper subset of $\\{0, \\ldots , q-1\\}$. Our proof is based on the linear algebra bound method and basic number theory.\n\n12 pages\n\nRelated Organizations\n\n  * Óbuda University\n\nHungary\n\nKeywords\n\nFOS: Mathematics, Mathematics - Combinatorics, 05D05, 12D99, 15A03, Combinatorics (math.CO)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBijective proofs of several conjectures on Jacobi permutations | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2608.11771)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Submitted 12 Aug 2026 ... Furthermore, we investigate pairs of statistics on André permutations and simsun permutations that are equidistributed with the pair $(\\asc, \\last)$(\\asc,\\last) on Jacobi permutations, where $\\asc$\\asc denotes the number of ascents of permutations. ... One of the primary objectives of the paper is to prove \"Conjecture 1.2,\" which asserts a symmetric joint distribution for two statistics on Jacobi permutations: the last letter, `last`, and a transformed version of the penultimate letter, `cpen`.\nSubmitted 12 Aug 2026\n\n# Bijective proofs of several conjectures on Jacobi permutations\n\nZL\n\nZhicong Lin\n\nYW\n\nYongzhou Wen\n\nSH\n\nSherry H.F. Yan\n\n## Abstract\n\nJacobi permutations, invented by Viennot in the context of the Jacobi elliptic functions, are counted by the Euler numbers. Recently, Henke, Hoffman, Stephens, Yuan, and Zhuang studied refined enumerations of Jacobi permutations and proposed three conjectures concerning the distribution of several statistics on Jacobi permutations. In this paper, we prove these conjectures by establishing explicit bijections involving increasing even trees, increasing binary trees, alternating permutations, and André permutations. One highlight of our results is a bijection between Jacobi permutations and André I permutations that transforms the pair of statistics $(\\Ascbot, \\last)$(\\Ascbot,\\last) to the pair of statistics $(\\Desbot, \\first)$(\\Desbot,\\first). Here the statistic $\\Ascbot$\\Ascbot (resp., $\\Desbot$\\Desbot) denotes the set of ascent bottoms (resp., descent bottoms) of permutations, and the statistic $\\first$\\first (resp., $\\last$\\last) denotes the first (resp., $\\last$\\last) letter of permutations. Furthermore, we investigate pairs of statistics on André permutations and simsun permutations that are equidistributed with the pair $(\\asc, \\last)$(\\asc,\\last) on Jacobi permutations, where $\\asc$\\asc denotes the number of ascents of permutations. Finally, we obtain a closed-form formula for the trivariate exponential generating function of Jacobi permutations with respect to the number of ascents and the numbers of letters smaller and larger than the last letter.\n\n## Introduction to Jacobi Permutations and Combinatorial Statistics\n\nEnumerative combinatorics often seeks to classify and count specific types of permutations based on their internal structural properties. A central figure in this field is the sequence of Euler numbers $E_n$E n​, which appear in the Taylor series expansion of the trigonometric functions tangent and secant:\n\n$$\\tan(x) + \\sec(x) = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} E_n \\frac{x^n}{n!}$$tan(x)+sec(x)=n=0∑∞​E n​n!x n​\n\nHistorically, these numbers were shown to count alternating permutations—sequences where the values alternately rise and fall. Over time, other classes of permutations have been discovered that are also counted by Euler numbers, including André permutations (of types I and II), simsun permutations, and Jacobi permutations.\n\nJacobi permutations were originally introduced by Viennot in the study of Jacobi elliptic functions. Recently, research by Henke, Hoffman, Stephens, Yuan, and Zhuang explored refined enumerations of these permutations. They investigated how specific \"statistics\"—numerical properties of a permutation like the value of the last entry or the number of ascents—distribute across the set. Their work established that the distribution of the last letter of a Jacobi permutation is governed by Entringer numbers $E_{n,k}$E n,k​, which are a refinement of the Euler numbers. Specifically, they found:\n\n$$E_{n,k} = |\\{\\pi \\in J_n \\mid \\text{last}(\\pi)=k\\}|$$E n,k​=∣{π∈J n​∣last(π)=k}∣\n\nwhere $J_n$J n​ is the set of Jacobi permutations of length $n$n. However, several deeper relationships remained conjectural. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImpartial Games and Nuclear Escalation Ladder (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/2606.29015)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Misère Quotients and Higher-Order Structure ...   * Asymmetric Capability (Partizan Games): Allowing $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}6 to differ by player transforms the game into the partizan regime, where surreal values and Conway's theory apply, and no efficient Nim-sum analog exists.\n## Misère Quotients and Higher-Order Structure\n\nThe key innovation tackling misère play is introduction of the misère quotient—a commutative monoid capturing the sum-behavior of states under loss-enforcing conventions. For $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}1, rigorous exhaustive induction yields a quotient structure $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}2 of order six and explicit loss set $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}3.\n\nThis algebraic structure, lacking group invertibility (nontrivial idempotents), underlies the failure of simple balance criteria in multi-theater misère sums. The analysis conjectures finiteness of the quotient for arbitrary $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}4, implying multi-theater stability is always decidable (modulo quotient computation). However, closed forms for $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}5, and for non-contiguous jump sets, remain open.\n\n## Extensions: Realism at the Expense of Tractability\n\nThree principal extensions are articulated:\n\n  * Asymmetric Capability (Partizan Games): Allowing $$\\mathcal{P} = \\{ r \\ge 0 : r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{m+1} \\}$$P={r≥0:r≡1(mod m+1)}6 to differ by player transforms the game into the partizan regime, where surreal values and Conway's theory apply, and no efficient Nim-sum analog exists.\n  * De-Escalation (Loopy Games): Permitting reductions in escalation (moving down the ladder) introduces cycles, requiring analysis with loopy and infinite combinatorial game theory. Draws and perpetual crises become possible.\n  * Three-Player/Multipolar Escalation: Moving beyond bipolar confrontation to scenarios such as U.S.-Russia-China confrontations invokes tripolar impartial game theory, where sum-invariants disappear entirely; outcome prediction depends on ad-hoc coalition and tie-breaking rules, reflecting lower analytic stability.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-04-01&page=117&range=56)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Mar 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2503.19696v1 ... We give also a generalization of the mentioned sequence, involving techniques that are not related to Fibbonaci numbers nor Beatty sequences, obtaining an infinite family of sequences that we conjecture that produce an infinite orthogonal array in which the number of symbols, trials and factors is countable infinite, and having an automorphism group isomorphic to Z acting regularly on the symbol set.\n  * Fibonacci-like partitions and their associated piecewise-defined permutations\n\nJon Asier Bárcena-Petisco, Luis Martínez, María Merino, Juan Manuel Montoya, Antonio Vera-López\n\nMar 26 2025 math.CO arXiv:2503.19696v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2503.19696, author = {Jon Asier Bárcena-Petisco and Luis Martínez and María Merino and Juan Manuel Montoya and Antonio Vera-López}, title = {{F}ibonacci-like partitions and their associated piecewise-defined permutations}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2503.19696}, note = {arXiv:2503.19696v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn this paper we introduce a family of partitions of the set of natural numbers, Fibonacci-like partitions. In particular, we introduce a Fibonacci-like partition in a number of parts corresponding to the Fibonacci numbers, the standard Fibonacci-like partitions of the first kind. That partition refines the well-known partition in two parts associated to the Wythoff sequences. In a similar way, we introduce another family of Fibonacci-like partitions of the natural numbers, with an initial segment removed, in a number of parts which also follows the Fibonacci sequence, the standard Fibonacci-like partitions of the second kind. We study some piecewise-defined permutations over Fibonacci-like partitions, which give both torsion-free and torsion elements in the symmetric group over the set of natural numbers. We give an application of Fibonacci-like partitions to the study of a sequence analyzed by A. Shapovalov and by B.J. Venkatachala. We give also a generalization of the mentioned sequence, involving techniques that are not related to Fibbonaci numbers nor Beatty sequences, obtaining an infinite family of sequences that we conjecture that produce an infinite orthogonal array in which the number of symbols, trials and factors is countable infinite, and having an automorphism group isomorphic to Z acting regularly on the symbol set.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nref/papers/nuclear_escalation_ladder_cgt/metadata.yaml at 785f5fb9c79783ac8edc6e77ad7393f7faf8e258 · jcorbin.tngl.sh/unum · Tangled (https://tangled.org/jcorbin.tngl.sh/unum/blob/785f5fb9c79783ac8edc6e77ad7393f7faf8e258/ref/papers/nuclear_escalation_ladder_cgt/metadata.yaml)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month; Policy interpretation and extensions left open for future work 20 pages: 12 21 code: none 22 urls: 23 arxiv_abs: https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29015 24 arxiv_html_v1: https://arxiv.org/html/2606.29015v1 25 arxiv_pdf: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.29015v1 26 doi: https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.29015 27 version_history: 28 # From arXiv's own submission-history block, read at fetch time.\n\nunum / ref / papers / nuclear_escalation_ladder_cgt / metadata.yaml\n\n11 kB 196 lines\n\n[Input] Wrap\n\nat 785f5fb9c79783ac8edc6e77ad7393f7faf8e258\n\nYAML\n\nView raw\n\nJoshua T Corbin (jcorbin+khaove@wunjo.org) devoker,skills: ref/*/metadata.yaml route:/fidelity_ledger: gate + research-ingest robustness (1958, 1976) 4w ago\n\n962c91d3\n\n` 1# Source metadata for the archived paper in this directory. 2title: \"Impartial Combinatorial Games and the Nuclear Escalati